---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import AutoLeaseCalculator from '@/components/tools/calc/AutoLeaseCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="auto-lease-calculator">
  <AutoLeaseCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the auto lease calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      A lease payment has two parts. The depreciation fee pays for the value the car loses:
      <code>(cap cost − residual) ÷ months</code>. The finance fee is the lease's interest:
      <code>(cap cost + residual) × money factor</code>. Sales tax is added to their sum in most US states.
    </p>
    <p>
      The adjusted cap cost is the agreed price plus fees in the lease, minus the down payment, trade-in and rebates.
      The residual is the MSRP times the residual percentage. The money factor is about the APR divided by 2,400.
    </p>
    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A car with an MSRP of 35,000 is agreed at 33,000. With 895 of fees, 2,000 down, a 58% residual, a money factor
      of 0.0025 (6% APR), 36 months and 7% tax: the cap cost is 31,895 and the residual is 20,300. The depreciation fee
      is 322.08, the finance fee is 130.49 and the tax is 31.68, so the payment is 484.25 a month. The 36 payments come
      to 17,433, or 19,433 with the down payment.
    </p>
    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      Excess mileage, wear charges and the end-of-lease disposition fee are not included. Some states tax the full
      price or the down payment. Ask the dealer for the money factor, residual and every fee in writing, and check the
      payment against this result.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
